# Public API base — absolute, including the /api prefix. The API shares the
# apps' parent domain (api.<root>), so its session cookie is first-party to
# every host under <root>; no reverse proxy needed.
#   Dev:  http://localhost:3000/api
#   Prod: https://api.ica2.xyz/api
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000/api
# Optional — absolute API base for server-side (SSR/prefetch) fetches when the
# public host isn't routable from the server itself (WSL/containers, LAN nip.io).
# Falls back to NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL when unset; leave unset in normal prod.
# API_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3000/api
# The diner-facing customer storefront — the app a table QR resolves to, and the
# host shown for a restaurant's menu (`<slug>.<root>`).
#   Prod: https://ica2.xyz  → menus at <slug>.ica2.xyz
NEXT_PUBLIC_CUSTOMER_URL=http://localhost:3002
# --- Optional: test on a real device over your LAN (e.g. a phone) ---
# Point the NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars above at your machine's LAN host, then expose the
# dev server and let SSR reach the API over localhost (the LAN IP isn't routable
# from the server):
#   HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0
#   ALLOWED_DEV_ORIGIN=192.168.x.x.nip.io
#   API_INTERNAL_URL=http://localhost:3000/api
#
# IMPORTANT (auth): use <ip>.nip.io — NOT the bare IP — and use the SAME host
# for NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL as the app and the API's ROOT_DOMAIN. The session
# cookie is set with Domain=ROOT_DOMAIN, and a browser only accepts a cookie
# whose domain matches the responding host. If the API is reached at a bare IP
# (e.g. 192.168.x.x) while ROOT_DOMAIN is 192.168.x.x.nip.io, the cookie is
# silently dropped and sign-in appears to do nothing. Keep them all on
# <ip>.nip.io:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.x.x.nip.io:3000/api
